02:33:17.890 --> 02:33:23.740
Omar Mansour: An educated guess, with reasoning. The Colonel? The Colonel. It isn't the kernel.

1689
02:33:24.030 --> 02:33:26.789
Omar Mansour: Why not keep it in process T itself?

1690
02:33:31.450 --> 02:33:34.279
Omar Mansour: Here's an educated reasoning, or guess.

1691
02:33:35.750 --> 02:33:40.299
Omar Mansour: What do we have here? Memory management information controlling the address space.

1692
02:33:43.420 --> 02:33:45.170
Omar Mansour: Scheduling priority.

1693
02:33:46.460 --> 02:33:58.590
Omar Mansour: You don't want process C to be able to, like, modify the… You don't want a process to modify its own address space. This will allow it to increase it and maybe infringe in the address space of another process. That has to be a privileged…

1694
02:34:00.150 --> 02:34:01.000
Omar Mansour: data.

1695
02:34:01.490 --> 02:34:05.379
Omar Mansour: Will not be accessible to an unprivileged user mode process.

1696
02:34:05.760 --> 02:34:10.850
Omar Mansour: To never be able to change its own memory space, or else it would defeat the whole protection mechanism.

1697
02:34:13.060 --> 02:34:16.810
Omar Mansour: What about… its location.

1698
02:34:18.780 --> 02:34:23.769
Omar Mansour: What about its location within queues? It should not be allowed to do that at all, to modify that at all.

1699
02:34:23.960 --> 02:34:27.529
Omar Mansour: It would never be allowed to modify its own schedule. Obviously, that's that.

1700
02:34:28.590 --> 02:34:29.770
Omar Mansour: Privileged.

1701
02:34:31.510 --> 02:34:32.530
Omar Mansour: task.

1702
02:34:32.760 --> 02:34:35.239
Omar Mansour: Same for the scheduling property.

1703
02:34:35.440 --> 02:34:36.670
Omar Mansour: This means…

1704
02:34:37.460 --> 02:34:45.250
Omar Mansour: Process control blocks are going to be 100% inside the kernel, in every operating system. They must be inside the kernel, okay?

1705
